routes.brief:
This reuses the candidate proposed by PAPER-SHANKAR-SHANKAR-TANG-ETAL-22, which plans heights of GSpin special divisors (modularity of generating series, Borcherds–Bruinier Green functions, arithmetic intersections on the integral models). Li–Zhang, Invent. Math. 228 (2022), 1353–1460, add intersections of n special divisors, in the direction of the unitary UnitaryKudlaRapoportCycles (PAPER-LI-ZHANG-22-B). Plan the orthogonal local theory in parallel with the unitary one, with the same statement shapes, and do not duplicate it.

Import:
- the GSpin Rapoport–Zink spaces, crystals, étale tensors, integral models and uniformization from OrthogonalIntegralModelsAndKugaSatake;
- the local densities of §3 and §8 from GeometryOfNumbersAndQuadraticArithmetic GN.3;
- Fourier analysis from AutomorphicLFunctionsAndLocalFactors AL.0, and the global Weil representation from MetaplecticAutomorphicForms MP.4;
- the invariant-distribution classification (§9.1) from MP.3;
- K-theory with supports (SchemeKTheoryOperations S.6–S.7, with Zhang 2021 Appendix B for formal schemes).

Construct:
- special cycles Z(L) and the flatness of Z(x) (Proposition 4.10.1);
- the Deligne–Lusztig varieties Y_W, the minuscule cycles V(Λ) ≅ Y_{W_Λ} and the Bruhat–Tits stratification;
- derived cycles and Int(L), properness, horizontal and vertical parts, and the cancellation law;
- quasi-canonical, Gross–Keating and primitive horizontal cycles, with their degrees.

The final theorems:
- Theorem 5.3.1 (the horizontal part) and Proposition 6.1.1 (the vertical part lies on N^red);
- Theorem 6.3.2 (the Tate conjecture for Y_d, via Lusztig);
- the horizontal identity (Theorem 7.2.2);
- the Deligne–Lusztig curve computations (Theorem 7.4.1, self-intersection −2q) and local modularity (Corollaries 7.5.3, 7.6.10);
- the analytic and geometric recurrences (Propositions 8.1.2, 9.2.3) and Theorem 9.3.2, hence Theorem 4.15.1;
- globally, incoherent Siegel Eisenstein series on Sp/Mp, Int_{T,p} and the semi-global Theorem 12.9.1.

Keep F = Q_p (p odd) for the geometric side; §§3, 8 and 9.1 hold for general F with odd residue characteristic. Keep the isotropic/anisotropic dichotomy of W explicit.

Tests:
- m = 4 against Gross–Keating (ε = +1) and Terstiege (ε = −1), including Example 4.15.3: ∂Den^−(L) = 3 − q;
- the values 1 − q, 1, 0 of Int_{V(Λ)};
- deg Z(M^♭)° for n = 2.

This reuses the candidate proposed by PAPER-CHARLES-16 and extended by PAPER-SHANKAR-SHANKAR-TANG-ETAL-22, which covers the GSpin Shimura datum, Kisin's models, Kuga–Satake and special endomorphisms. Li–Zhang, Invent. Math. 228 (2022), add the local basic-locus counterpart.

Construct:
- the GSpin Rapoport–Zink space RZ_G of Hodge type (Howard–Pappas 2017; Kim 2018): local unramified Shimura–Hodge data, the framing object with crystalline Tate tensors, formal smoothness of relative dimension n − 1, the J_b-action, the connected components and the connected space N^ε_n;
- the exceptional isomorphisms for m ≤ 5 (Example 4.5.3);
- the special quasi-endomorphisms V ≅ V^ε_m, the projector π_crys and the crystal V_crys with its Hodge line (Kim Thm. 4.9.1), and Grothendieck–Messing for N and Z(L) (Lemma 4.6.2);
- integral étale Tate tensors (Kim Thm. 7.1.6), special endomorphisms over O_K and Lemma 5.5.2 (using Tate's theorem from R07.1);
- globally, the special cycles Z(x, g)_K and the semi-global Z(T, ϕ_K) on Kisin's model (finite unramified, supersingular support for r = n), and the p-adic uniformization Θ with Proposition 12.7.1 (Howard–Pappas Thm. 7.2.4, Kim 2018b).

Keep p > 2 and F = Q_p, as the source does; Remark 1.3.3 leaves relative GSpin RZ spaces over general p-adic fields open.

Tests:
- m = 3 (Lubin–Tate) and m = 4 (a product of Lubin–Tate spaces, and M^HB).

routes.reason:
GN.3 constructs local representation densities. This source adds the quadratic (orthogonal) local-density package:
- densities against H^ε_m for both ε, via Cho–Yamauchi weighted lattice counting (Lemma 3.3.2) and the finite-field isometry counts behind it (Lemma 3.2.1);
- the normalized local Siegel series in odd and even corank, with Ikeda's functional equations and the induction formula;
- horizontal lattices;
- the weighted counting identities for full-type lattices (§8.2) and the valuation-reduction Lemma 9.3.1.
